<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.15.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00337</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.15.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>2a915bb9-a73b-498e-ab78-d42012ecdef9</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <exercise id="_87ea4671-e924-4106-8958-0fb20fd70e4b" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">If y varies directly as <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>14</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. Find the equation that relates <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Correct! <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00004">Try again. Revisit activity 1.15.1.</para>
          <para id="para-00005">The correct answer is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="e801f482-f79e-445c-a137-4b9b16a764c9" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">The price, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>P</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, that Eric pays for gas varies directly with the number of gallons, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, he buys. It costs him $50 to buy 20 gallons of gas. Choose the equation that relates <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>P</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00007">Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para id="para-00008"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>25</m:mn><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00009"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>2.5</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00010"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>G</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>2.5</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00011"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>2.5</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00012"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mrow><m:mn>2.5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00013">Correct! <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00014">Try again. Revisit activities 1.15.2 and 1.15.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00015">The correct answer is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="db54efcd-a1a1-4135-83cc-fd19b4ae9897" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00016">Joseph is traveling on a road trip. The distance, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, he travels before stopping for lunch varies directly with the speed, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>v</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, he travels. He can travel 120 miles at a speed of 60 mph. How many miles would he travel before stopping for lunch at a rate of 65 mph?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00017">Correct! 130 miles</para>
          <para id="para-00018">Try again. Revisit activities 1.15.2 and 1.15.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00019">The correct answer is 130 miles.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="c88ca437-735d-4652-8b7a-c31bdc723a57" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00020">The mass of a liquid, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>M</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, varies directly with its volume, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>V</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. A liquid with mass 16 kilograms has a volume of 2 liters. What is the volume, in liters, of this liquid if its mass is 128 kilograms?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00021">Correct! 16 liters. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>M</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mi>V</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00022">Try again. Revisit activities 1.15.2 and 1.15.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00023">The correct answer is 16 liters. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>M</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mi>V</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_968a0e07-07a0-4dfb-ab3f-a99e74be221f" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00024">The length, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>L</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, that a spring stretches varies directly with a weight, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>W</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, placed at the end of the spring. When Sarah placed a 10-pound watermelon on a hanging scale, the spring stretched 5 inches. What weight of watermelon, in pounds, would stretch the spring 6 inches?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00025">Correct! 12 pounds, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>L</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>W</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00026">Try again. Revisit activities 1.15.2 and 1.15.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00027">The correct answer is 12 pounds, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>L</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mi>W</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="f84ffff3-68b7-4dea-9903-7a1ca6ab2545" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00028">The maximum load a beam, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>M</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, will support varies directly with the square of the diagonal of the beam’s cross-section, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>d</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>. A beam with diagonal 6 inch will support a maximum load of 108 pounds. What load, in pounds, will a beam with a 10-inch diagonal support?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00029">Correct! 300 pounds. Use the direct variation equation, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>M</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>d</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00030">Try again. Revisit activities 1.15.2 and 1.15.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00031">The correct answer is 300 pounds. Use the direct variation equation, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>M</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>d</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_0ff5da75-2446-49a8-bd92-332f581059a2" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00032">The number of calories, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, burned varies directly with the amount of time, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, spent exercising. Arnold burned 312 calories in 65 minutes exercising. How many calories would he burn if he exercises for 90 minutes?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00033">Correct! He would burn 432 calories.</para>
          <para id="para-00034">Try again. Revisit activities 1.15.2 and 1.15.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00035">The correct answer is is a. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>M</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mi>V</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>; b. 16 liters.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
